#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc0a5e10-c402-54cb-80e6-ba4fcc3e9df4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Written in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "50074a6d-1ca5-580b-a716-0a69bcae773d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4166" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^3 5^n}$?" :question/worked-solution "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $1$ to $3$. So, computing the terms for $n=1,2,3$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(n = 1)^(3)5^{n} &= 5^{1} + 5^{2} + 5^{3} \\\\\n&= 5 + 25 + 125 \\\\\n&= 155\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078288 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50fb047f-8c8d-56a6-9093-29a68cd89a90" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Sums Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9891d5c-d928-589e-a461-3f0b39fb1b66" :question/math-academy-id "e-4168" :question/problem "Express $S=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60$ in sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the terms of the corresponding sequence\n\n$4, \\quad 8, \\quad 12, \\quad, \\ldots, \\quad 56, \\quad 60$.\nare multiples of $4$, and since\n\n$$\n60\\div 4 = 15\n$$\n\nthere are $15$ terms in total.\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence has the form\n$a_n =4n$,\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to\n\n$$\nn=15\\mathbin{.}\n$$\n\nIndeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 4(1) = 4 \\\\\na_{2} &= 4(2) = 8 \\\\\na_{3} &= 4(3) = 12 \\\\\n&= ⋮ \\\\\na_{14} &= 4(14) = 56 \\\\\na_{15} &= 4(15) = 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} S=4+8+12+\\cdots+56+60=\\sum_{n=1}^{15} 4n. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078569 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "497072ab-d582-5896-b8a2-12bf1f07c8e9" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding Sums Written in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "be3fc348-da73-5858-bea0-6023bf044e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-4169" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=2}^{4} (2n-3)$ as a sum of three terms." :question/worked-solution "The sum takes integer values of $n$ from $2$ to $4$. So, we compute the terms for $n=2,3,4$ and write the sum, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=2}^{4}(2n-3)&= \\overbrace{\\big(2(2)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,2}+\\overbrace{\\big(2(3)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,3}+\\overbrace{\\big(2(4)-3\\big)}^{\\large n\\,=\\,4} \\\\[-5pt] &=(4-3)+(6-3)+(8-3) \\\\[5pt] &=1+3+5 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078570 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc0a5e10-c402-54cb-80e6-ba4fcc3e9df4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions Written in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a83cbf86-1ce6-5218-a7d9-b03677f42276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077307} {:answer/id #uuid "bddf2f00-d2b8-584a-887a-645b04f94477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077308} {:answer/id #uuid "01ef71ea-f294-58cc-9ca0-aa24f839ce9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077309} {:answer/id #uuid "31cdccc3-503f-5be6-a164-b0d163dfa45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077310} {:answer/id #uuid "5e322700-e957-5ea9-87fa-221887a480cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077311}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5b39ae9a-e902-5928-9d54-59a495241588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 175921